<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Never economise here</span><strong>Waterproofing is the one line item you must never trim.</strong> A failed membrane means seepage into your own bedroom wall — or your downstairs neighbour's ceiling — and redoing it later costs 3–4× more because everything above it must be broken again. Insist on a proper waterproofing system with curing time and a written warranty.</div>
<h2>07.Bedroom, Living Room &amp; Other Spaces</h2>
<h3>Bedroom renovation: ₹1.5–4 L per room</h3>
<p>Typically a wardrobe (₹80,000–2 L depending on size and finish), a headboard or panelled feature wall (₹30,000–80,000), false ceiling with cove (₹25,000–60,000), painting, and electrical updates. Add ₹40,000–1 L if flooring changes.</p>
<h3>Living + dining renovation: ₹2–6 L</h3>
<p>TV unit (₹50,000–1.5 L), false ceiling with layered lighting (₹50,000–1.2 L), feature wall (₹30,000–1 L), crockery unit, painting, and switches/points. This zone rewards spending on lighting more than on any single furniture piece.</p>
<h3>Frequently forgotten line items</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Balconies:</strong> deck tiles, seating, and greenery — ₹30,000–1 L per balcony.</li>
  <li><strong>Pooja unit:</strong> ₹25,000–80,000 depending on carving and back-panel work.</li>
  <li><strong>Utility area:</strong> washer plumbing, storage, and dado tiling — ₹20,000–60,000.</li>
  <li><strong>Main door refurbishment:</strong> polish or replacement — ₹8,000–60,000.</li>
  <li><strong>Safety/handover items:</strong> deep cleaning, pest treatment, touch-ups — ₹10,000–25,000.</li>
</ul>
<h2>08.The 7 Factors That Swing Your Cost Most</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Age of the property.</strong> Homes older than 15 years usually need full electrical rewiring (old wiring can't handle modern loads) and plumbing replacement — a ₹1.5–4 L layer newer flats skip.</li>
  <li><strong>Whether wet areas move.</strong> Relocating kitchens or bathrooms multiplies civil and plumbing cost, and in apartments may not be permitted at all.</li>
  <li><strong>Material grade.</strong> The essential-to-premium jump adds 30–60% per item — worth it in kitchens and master wardrobes, rarely in guest rooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Flooring decisions.</strong> Laying new tile over old (where feasible) costs a fraction of breaking and re-laying — but raises floor height. Full re-flooring for a 2BHK: ₹1.5–4 L.</li>
  <li><strong>Society rules &amp; access.</strong> Restricted work hours, no service lift, debris rules, and refundable deposits all extend timelines and labour cost in gated communities.</li>
  <li><strong>Living in vs moving out.</strong> Occupied renovations run 15–25% slower. Compare that cost with two months' rent nearby before deciding.</li>
  <li><strong>Scope creep.</strong> The famous "while we're at it..." Every mid-project addition is priced without competition and delays everything behind it. Freeze scope before demolition day.</li>
</ol>
<h2>09.Hidden Costs: The Post-Demolition Surprises</h2>
<p>These are the items that most commonly appear <em>after</em> the quote is signed. Ask about every one of them upfront — and read our full guide to <a href="/blogs/hidden-costs-home-interiors/">hidden costs in home interiors</a> for the new-interiors equivalents:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Debris disposal beyond estimate</strong> — old flats often yield 30–50% more debris than assumed.</li>
  <li><strong>Seepage &amp; waterproofing repairs</strong> discovered behind bathroom and kitchen tiles.</li>
  <li><strong>Termite treatment</strong> if trails are found during demolition (₹8,000–25,000).</li>
  <li><strong>Electrical load upgrade</strong> — new ACs and appliances may need a sanctioned-load increase and new MCB distribution.</li>
  <li><strong>Society deposits &amp; charges</strong> — refundable, but they lock up ₹10,000–50,000 of cash flow.</li>
  <li><strong>Furniture shifting &amp; storage</strong> during work (₹15,000–60,000 for a typical 2BHK over two months).</li>
  <li><strong>GST</strong> — confirm whether every quote you compare includes it.</li>
  <li><strong>Deep cleaning &amp; touch-up</strong> at handover, often excluded from base quotes.</li>
</ul>
<h2>10.10 Ways to Cut Renovation Cost — Without Cutting Corners</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Keep wet points where they are.</strong> The single largest saving available.</li>
  <li><strong>Renovate in the right order, once.</strong> Civil → plumbing/electrical → waterproofing → tiling → carpentry → painting. Out-of-order work gets redone.</li>
  <li><strong>Retile over existing floors</strong> where height and structure allow.</li>
  <li><strong>Refinish instead of replace</strong> — solid wood doors, good granite, and structurally sound wardrobe carcasses can be re-laminated or polished for 30–40% of replacement cost.</li>
  <li><strong>Spend by zone, not uniformly</strong> — premium in kitchen and master wardrobe, standard elsewhere (the hybrid logic from our <a href="/blogs/essential-vs-luxury-interiors/">essential vs luxury guide</a>).</li>
  <li><strong>Buy sanitaryware and appliances during sale seasons</strong> and have the contractor install — but confirm warranty responsibility in writing first.</li>
  <li><strong>Freeze the design before demolition.</strong> Mid-project changes are the most expensive purchases you'll ever make.</li>
  <li><strong>Move out if the project exceeds 6 weeks</strong> — the speed gain usually beats the rent.</li>
  <li><strong>Get itemised quotes only.</strong> Lump-sum quotes hide both padding and omissions.</li>
  <li><strong>Never skip waterproofing, wiring, or ply grade to save money.</strong> These are the three savings that always return as bigger bills.</li>
</ol>
